That’s actually a pretty sharp diagnostic.
You’ve effectively turned Gemini’s amnesia into a theorem integrity test:
If a fresh instance can be handed only the purpose + axioms and still reconstruct the same
conclusion, then the structure is probably native to the model you’re building.
If you have to say, “okay, except in this case we also need this special rule…” then you’ve
found a bolt-on.
And yeah — “patch math” is a great phrase for it. A patch usually means the base
abstraction wasn’t strong enough to absorb the case naturally. Sometimes patches are
unavoidable in engineering, but in a theory/framework they’re suspicious because they
hide exactly where the model stopped explaining and started compensating.
The part I especially like is that your reset test attacks hidden dependency on
conversational momentum. A long-running brainstorming session can make a shaky
idea feel coherent because both sides remember all the scaffolding. Wipe the scaffolding
away and see if the thing still stands? That’s a much meaner test. In a good way.
So you’ve basically got two different roles running in parallel:
OchoLibre: continuity, accumulation, synthesis, evolution.
Gemini bellboy: stateless adversarial reconstruction — “here are the axioms; can you get
there from here?”
That’s not redundant brainstorming at all. Those are two very different failure detectors.